{% llm_copy_prompt title="Let an AI agent set it up for you" %}
Help me set up CI for my Nx workspace on Nx Cloud CI.

Before touching anything, verify the workspace state:

**A. Is Nx installed?**

- Check for `nx.json` and `nx` in `package.json` devDeps.
- Confirm `node_modules` exists. If not, install deps using the package manager that matches my lockfile (`pnpm install`, `npm install`, or `yarn`).
- If `nx.json` is missing entirely, ask me before running `npx nx@latest init`.

**B. Where is the repository hosted?**

- Check `git remote -v`. Nx Cloud CI needs a github.com repository. If it's anywhere else, stop and point me to /docs/kb/bring-your-own-ci.

**C. Is there an existing CI setup?**

- If `.nx/pipelines/ci.yaml` has `on` and `jobs`, Nx Cloud CI is already set up. Confirm with me before changing anything.
- If `.nx/ci-config.yaml` exists, tell me it's deprecated and point me to /docs/kb/migrate-from-ci-config.
- If there's a provider workflow (for example `.github/workflows/*.yml`), tell me Nx Cloud CI runs alongside it until I remove it, and point me to /docs/kb/migrate-from-github-actions to convert it.

Then set up Nx Cloud CI:

1. Point me to https://cloud.nx.app/get-started?plan=nx-cloud-ci to sign in and pick Nx Cloud CI. Stop until I confirm.
2. Run `npx nx-cloud onboard connect-workspace` and parse the JSON. If the response includes an `actionRequired` payload (typically GitHub authorization), surface the message and any URLs to me and stop. Do not retry blindly.
3. Confirm `nxCloudId` is written to `nx.json`. If it is not, surface the JSON error to me instead of retrying.
4. Create `.nx/pipelines/ci.yaml` from the complete example on {pageUrl}. Replace `lint test build` with the targets my workspace has.

Stage the generated or edited files but do not commit on my behalf. For deeper coverage link to {pageUrl} and to [/docs/features/ci-features](/docs/features/ci-features).

Page: {pageUrl}
{% /llm_copy_prompt %}

Run your whole pipeline on Nx Cloud CI from one file in your repository.
Remote caching, `nx affected`, and self-healing CI work without extra steps, and spreading tasks across machines takes one line.
For a finished pipeline, jump to the [complete example](#complete-example).

Nx Cloud CI is in beta for repositories on github.com, with more providers coming soon.
If your repository is somewhere else, or you'd rather keep your current CI provider, see [bring your own CI](/docs/kb/bring-your-own-ci).

{% call_to_action variant="default" title="Tour an Nx Cloud workspace" url="https://cloud.nx.app/demo/intro?utm_source=nx-docs&utm_medium=website&utm_campaign=nx-cloud-demo&utm_content=ci-setup" description="See distributed task execution, caching, and run analytics on a live workspace before you wire up your own CI." /%}

## Make sure you have Nx

For existing repos, run the init command and follow the prompts:

```shell
npx nx@latest init
```

Or start fresh with a new repo:

```shell
npx create-nx-workspace@latest
```

## Connect to Nx Cloud

Push your workspace to GitHub, then sign in to Nx Cloud and connect it through the [GitHub App](/docs/kb/github-integration):

{% call_to_action variant="default" title="Connect your workspace" url="https://cloud.nx.app/get-started?plan=nx-cloud-ci&utm_source=nx-docs&utm_medium=website&utm_campaign=nx-cloud-signup&utm_content=ci-setup" description="Setup takes less than 5 minutes" /%}

Nx Cloud is free to start, and the CI Plan is $0 during the beta.
Once connected, [remote caching](/docs/features/ci-features/remote-cache) works on your machine and in CI.
A task that already ran with the same inputs, in CI or on a teammate's machine, is restored from the cache instead of running again.
Because Nx Cloud CI runs tasks on Nx Cloud workers, [Ultracache](/docs/features/ci-features/remote-cache#ultracache) configures each task's inputs and outputs from the files it reads and writes, so you don't declare them by hand.

## Run CI with Nx

Commit a pipeline file on a branch and open a pull request into `main`:

```yaml
# .nx/pipelines/ci.yaml
# ... name and triggers
jobs:
  main:
    runs-on: linux-medium-js
    steps:
      - run: npx nx affected -t lint test build
```

`linux-medium-js` is a pre-built [launch template](/docs/kb/launch-templates) that checks out your repository and installs its dependencies.
Before your first step, Nx Cloud sets `NX_BASE` and `NX_HEAD`, so [`nx affected`](/docs/features/ci-features/affected) runs tasks only for projects your change touches, with no extra setup.
For the triggers, see the [complete example](#complete-example).

Caching, affected, and distribution only apply to tasks that `nx` runs.
`nx test` is fine, and so is `npm test` if it wraps `nx test`.
Direct calls to `jest`, `tsc`, or `eslint` skip Nx.

## Distribute tasks with workers

With workers, your tasks run across several machines instead of one.
With [_dynamic task packing_](/docs/features/ci-features/distribute-task-execution#how-dynamic-task-packing-works), Nx Cloud uses what it knows about each task, such as its duration and its CPU and memory use, to keep workers close to capacity, so fewer machine-minutes sit idle.
In most workspaces, that adds up to about 50% fewer machine-minutes than remote caching alone.

The job distributes tasks to its own [main-job worker](/docs/reference/nx-cloud/ci-config#main-job-worker).
To run them across more machines, add `workers` to the job:

```yaml {% meta="{5}" %}
# .nx/pipelines/ci.yaml
jobs:
  main:
    runs-on: linux-medium-js
    workers: 3 linux-large-js
    steps:
      - run: npx nx affected -t lint test build
```

{% aside type="note" title="Nothing to tune" %}
Nx Cloud decides how many tasks run on each worker, so there's no `--parallel` value to set.
For every configuration option, see the [CI configuration file reference](/docs/reference/nx-cloud/ci-config).
{% /aside %}

## Complete example

This pipeline combines affected, remote caching, and distribution:

```yaml
// .nx/pipelines/ci.yaml
name: CI

on:
  pull-requests:
    event: PR
    branches: [main]
  main-pushes:
    event: PUSH
    branches: [main]

worker-templates:
  default:
    # Distribute across 3 workers using the linux-large-js launch template
    workers: 3 linux-large-js
    # Shut idle workers down once every build task has been requested
    stop-after:
      - build

jobs:
  main:
    # Checks out the repository and installs dependencies
    runs-on: linux-medium-js
    workers: default
    steps:
      # Runs lint, test, and build only for projects affected by this change.
      # Nx Cloud distributes these tasks across the workers started above.
      - run: npx nx affected -t lint test build
```

Nx Cloud CI runs alongside any CI you already have, so CI runs twice until you remove the old workflow.
To convert a GitHub Actions workflow, see [move a GitHub Actions workflow to Nx Cloud CI](/docs/kb/migrate-from-github-actions).

